         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Introduction\n\nIn this class we are going to learn the multiplication table of five (5).\n\nThis is a really important multiplication table, because it has a direct relationship with our bodies: our hands and our feet are natural groups of five. And this is not the only reason it is special: the results of the table of five follow a pattern that is very easy to recognize, and we will discover it in this class.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "ea68c7be-96f8-4036-ae33-37df03d11d54",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "ea68c7be-96f8-4036-ae33-37df03d11d54",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "happy",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "240800f3-ede4-4deb-9d4e-915a3a03fa68",
               "text": "History Tracers Team",
               "page": "https://github.com/historytracers/historytracers/tree/master/src/images",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2024",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            },
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "3bc9800d-aa41-4930-84f4-749eb6e40657",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=3bc9800d-aa41-4930-84f4-749eb6e40657",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### My Body\n\nIn previous classes we learned that our body has parts that can be counted two by two: two hands, two eyes, two feet.\n\nBut this is not the only way to see multiplication tables in our body. We can also observe the numbers of our fingers and toes growing five by five.\n\nOne hand has 5 fingers: 5 × 1 = 5. Two hands have 10 fingers: 5 × 2 = 10. One foot has 5 toes, and two feet have 10 toes. Count them five by five: 5, 10, 15, 20!",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "51819a98-f56a-4a7b-9017-f4fd97a699e4",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "51819a98-f56a-4a7b-9017-f4fd97a699e4",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "thinking",
         "source_menu": [],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Thinking\n\nWhen I raise all my fingers, I have 10 fingers, and I have already completed one ten. Does it mean that, in the table of five, the units digit can only alternate between two values?",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "83c2a47e-5615-4762-8add-8f210e85b5e9",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": "yes",
         "score": 2,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "83c2a47e-5615-4762-8add-8f210e85b5e9",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "nerd",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "3bc9800d-aa41-4930-84f4-749eb6e40657",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=3bc9800d-aa41-4930-84f4-749eb6e40657",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Times 5\n\nBecause the units digit can only have values between 0 and 9, the table of five can only show two different digits in the units place.\n\nThe first one is zero, where the multiplication table starts: 5 × 0 = 0. The second one is five, because the next time we add 5, the units return to zero and one more ten appears: 5, 10, 15, 20, 25...\n\nObserve:",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<table style=\"margin:0 auto; border-collapse:collapse; text-align:center;\"><tr><td style=\"padding:4px 22px;\">5 × 1 = 5</td><td style=\"padding:4px 22px;\">5 × 2 = 10</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:4px 22px;\">5 × 3 = 15</td><td style=\"padding:4px 22px;\">5 × 4 = 20</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:4px 22px;\">5 × 5 = 25</td><td style=\"padding:4px 22px;\"></td></tr></table>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "The units digit alternates between 5 and 0, while the tens grow one by one.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "5fdb48f4-db33-4053-9b9a-eff92018ac27",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "5fdb48f4-db33-4053-9b9a-eff92018ac27",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "nerd",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "3bc9800d-aa41-4930-84f4-749eb6e40657",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=3bc9800d-aa41-4930-84f4-749eb6e40657",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Visualizing\n\nTo help you visualize this, let us count objects that are present 10 times.\n\nSelect one of the objects in the selector below. Then click on the multiplications one by one and verify how many of these objects are shown when each group holds 5 of them.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "c0463101-87f2-431c-8963-c94b7b4656dc",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "c0463101-87f2-431c-8963-c94b7b4656dc",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "inlove",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "3bc9800d-aa41-4930-84f4-749eb6e40657",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=3bc9800d-aa41-4930-84f4-749eb6e40657",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### A Rule\n\nConsidering that we are always advancing 5 by 5, it is possible to understand this multiplication table as:\n\n**N + N + N + N + N = 5 × N**",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "f6df0828-4e6e-4d45-b023-4b3bd08daf7a",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "f6df0828-4e6e-4d45-b023-4b3bd08daf7a",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "nerd",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "3bc9800d-aa41-4930-84f4-749eb6e40657",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=3bc9800d-aa41-4930-84f4-749eb6e40657",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Reading the Rule\n\nLike the multiplication tables before, we can extract important information from this new recipe:\n\n**N + N + N + N + N = 5 × N**\n\nor, written with the multiplication first: **5 × N = N + N + N + N + N**\n\nRead it aloud: “five times N equals N plus N plus N plus N plus N.”\n\n- The product is always N added to itself five times.\n- **N** is a placeholder for any number you like, so one rule covers every multiplication by five at once.\n- For example, if **N** is 3, then 5 × 3 = 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 = 15.\n- If **N** is 0, the product is 0; if **N** is 1, the product is 5.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "045c8d63-1e72-4f56-9541-ce577af79c1d",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "045c8d63-1e72-4f56-9541-ce577af79c1d",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "party",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "3bc9800d-aa41-4930-84f4-749eb6e40657",
